kim - kernel integrity monitor (version @VERSION@)
=================================================

Continuously verifies the running kernel: kernel text, the syscall tables and
the text/rodata of every loaded module are tagged (AES-256-GCM-SIV, VAES) on
the P-cores of a hybrid Intel CPU and compared with a baseline learned at
start-up. Legitimate runtime patching (static keys, ftrace) is decoded and
reported as low severity; unexplained code changes, writable kernel text and
syscall-table changes are critical.

Supported: Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, Intel Core 12th gen (Alder Lake) and newer.

Packages
  kim-dkms    kernel_extractor module source, built by DKMS for every kernel
  kim-daemon  /usr/sbin/kimd, kim.service, /etc/kim/kimd.conf

Secure Boot
  DKMS signs the module with /var/lib/shim-signed/mok/MOK.priv/.der. If that
  key is not enrolled: sudo mokutil --import /var/lib/shim-signed/mok/MOK.der,
  reboot, choose "Enroll MOK". To use a key you already enrolled, copy it there
  (MOK.priv mode 0600, root) before installing kim-dkms.

Use
  systemctl status kim            running? baseline learned?
  journalctl -u kim -f            events as they happen
  ausearch -m TRUSTED_APP -ts today   audit records (warn and above)
  /etc/kim/kimd.conf              options; monitor-only by default

Enforcement (-e in KIMD_OPTS) raises lockdown to "confidentiality" and sets
kernel.modules_disabled=1 on a critical event. Both are one-way until reboot.
Test it in a VM first.

Performance (i5-12450H, back-to-back scans, 2026-11): ~13 GB/s on battery,
~15.6 GB/s on AC. At the default one scan per second a scan of ~41 MiB takes
about 3 ms (the pipeline is busy <1% of the time).
